# TRACK mobile — environment variables (template, no secrets).
#
# Expo (SDK 58) loads env files per command mode, first match wins:
#   development: .env.development.local > .env.local > .env.development > .env
#   production:  .env.production.local  > .env.local > .env.production  > .env
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are INLINED into the JS bundle — never put secrets
# here; anyone with the APK can read them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Case 1 — dev against local Django (default, nothing to do)
#   .env.development (committed) already points at http://localhost:8000/api.
#
# Case 2 — dev from Android emulator / iOS simulator / physical device
#   Create `mobile/.env.local` (gitignored, machine-specific):
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8000/api          # Android emulator
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.10:8000/api      # physical device
#   And start Django with the matching allowed host:
#     DJANGO_EXTRA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=192.168.1.10
#
# Case 3 — local release bundle (`npx expo export -p android`)
#   Create `mobile/.env.production.local` (gitignored):
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://<deployed-backend-host>/api
#   Release builds refuse to run with a missing or localhost URL.
#
# Case 4 — EAS build (cloud APK/AAB)
#   Set EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL in `mobile/eas.json` under the build profile's
#   `env` (or in the EAS dashboard environment variables). EAS never uploads
#   gitignored .env files, so the value MUST come from one of those two.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL — optional, only for live messaging
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The messaging socket is served by the ASGI application, not by the WSGI REST
# API. Same origin in production (one nginx/Daphne fronting both), different
# ports in development, because `manage.py runserver` cannot upgrade a socket:
#
#   daphne config.asgi:application -p 8010 -b 127.0.0.1    # backend, dev
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL=http://localhost:8010               # simulator / web
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8010                # Android emulator
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL=http://<LAN-IP>:8010                # physical device
#
# Unset, the socket reuses EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL's origin — correct for any
# deployment where the API host also serves /api/ws/messaging/. Leaving it unset
# costs only liveness: the inbox and threads still load over REST, so nothing
# arrives until you pull to refresh.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_URL=
